<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">7669562</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2033912</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Microsatellite instability in human prostate cancer.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Watanabe</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Imai</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Shiraishi</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Shimazaki</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kotake</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Yatani</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>Department of Pathology, Mie University School of Medicine, Japan.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>9</month><year>1995</year></pub-date><volume>72</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>562</fpage><lpage>564</lpage><abstract><p>Microsatellite instability (MSI) was examined at 36 loci, and found in 9 (43%) of the 21 prostatic cancers. A loss of heterozygosity had occurred in five cases (24%). MSI did not correlate with clinical stage, but might play a role in the development of a subset of prostate cancers.</p><sec sec-type="scanned-figures"><title>Images</title><fig id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00043-0047-a" xlink:role="563"/></fig></sec></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00043-0046.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="562" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00043-0047.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="563" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00043-0048.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="564" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>